---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Understanding AI API Costs: A Complete Guide"
  description="How AI API pricing actually works — tokens, context windows, fine-tuning, and embeddings — with links to calculators for each."
>
  <div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto px-4 py-16 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <p class="text-sm text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 font-medium mb-2">Guide</p>
    <h1>Understanding AI API Costs: A Complete Guide</h1>
    <p>
      AI API pricing looks simple at first glance — a price per million tokens — but the actual
      monthly bill for a real application depends on several interacting factors most people don't
      account for until the invoice arrives. This guide walks through each cost driver and links to
      a calculator for working out your specific numbers.
    </p>

    <h2>Tokens: the actual unit you're paying for</h2>
    <p>
      Every request and response is billed in tokens, not words or characters — roughly ¾ of a word
      per token for English text, though this varies by language and content type. Input and output
      tokens are usually priced differently, with output typically costing 3–5x more than input
      because generation is more computationally expensive than reading a prompt.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before estimating cost for a feature, get an accurate token count for your typical prompts and
      expected responses using the <a href="/tools/token-counter">Token Counter</a>, then calculate
      exact per-request cost with the <a href="/tools/quick-request-cost">AI Request Cost Quick Calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Context window: what you're paying to keep "in view"</h2>
    <p>
      A model's context window is the maximum number of tokens it can consider at once — your
      system prompt, conversation history, and any retrieved documents all count against it, and
      you pay for every token sent, not just the new message. A chat feature that resends full
      conversation history on every turn can rack up cost far faster than the visible message length
      suggests.
    </p>
    <p>
      Check whether your content fits a given model's window with the
      <a href="/tools/context-window-calculator">Context Window Calculator</a>, and project how a
      growing conversation compounds in cost using the
      <a href="/tools/chat-history-calculator">Chat History Token Calculator</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Fine-tuning and embeddings: the less obvious cost centers</h2>
    <p>
      Fine-tuning a model involves both a one-time training cost and a per-token inference cost that
      can differ from the base model's pricing — sometimes higher, since custom models often run on
      dedicated capacity. Estimate both sides with the
      <a href="/tools/fine-tune-cost-estimator">Fine-Tuning Cost Estimator</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're building a RAG (retrieval-augmented generation) pipeline, embedding your documents
      is a separate, often-overlooked cost — every chunk of every document needs to be embedded once
      (and re-embedded if content changes), plus ongoing storage in a vector database. The
      <a href="/tools/embedding-cost-calculator">Embedding Cost Calculator</a> and
      <a href="/tools/vector-db-comparator">Vector Database Cost Comparator</a> cover ingestion and
      storage respectively.
    </p>

    <h2>A practical way to estimate a new feature's cost</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Draft a realistic example prompt and expected response for your feature.</li>
      <li>Count tokens for both with the Token Counter.</li>
      <li>Calculate per-request cost with your target model's pricing.</li>
      <li>Multiply by your expected monthly request volume for a full estimate.</li>
      <li>If conversations have history, project growth with the Chat History Token Calculator rather than assuming a flat per-request cost.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      This sequence catches the two most common estimation mistakes: underestimating token count
      from a vague guess, and forgetting that conversational context compounds cost over a session
      rather than staying flat.
    </p>

